1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,667
This is ECE 358, video 14a. The topic is IPv4 addressing, part 1: addresses, subnets and masks.

2
00:00:11,117 --> 00:00:16,877
After this video, you can convert an IP address between dotted-decimal and binary.

3
00:00:17,327 --> 00:00:19,695
You can find the subnets of a network.

4
00:00:20,145 --> 00:00:23,345
You can read a prefix and write its network mask.

5
00:00:23,795 --> 00:00:32,627
And you can explain the network ID and the broadcast address. Video 14b computes them and solves the question bank.

6
00:00:33,077 --> 00:00:45,109
This network has one router in the middle and seven hosts. Each label is an IPv4 address. An IPv4 address is a 32-bit identifier.

7
00:00:45,559 --> 00:00:51,233
An IP address does not identify a device. It identifies an interface.

8
00:00:51,683 --> 00:00:59,726
An interface is the connection between a host or a router and a physical link. The orange dots are the interfaces.

9
00:01:00,176 --> 00:01:19,696
A router usually has many interfaces. This router has three interfaces, so it has three IP addresses: 223.1.1.4, 223.1.2.9 and 223.1.3.27.

10
00:01:20,146 --> 00:01:33,906
A host usually has one or two interfaces. For example, a laptop can have a wired Ethernet interface and a wireless interface. Then it has two IP addresses, one for each interface.

11
00:01:34,356 --> 00:01:46,153
Inside the network, an IPv4 address is a string of 32 bits. Here is the address 223.1.1.1 in binary.

12
00:01:46,603 --> 00:01:53,601
To write it for people, split the 32 bits into 4 groups of 8 bits. Each group is one byte.

13
00:01:54,051 --> 00:02:01,240
Write each byte as a decimal number, and put a dot between the numbers. This is dotted-decimal notation.

14
00:02:01,690 --> 00:02:12,122
To convert one byte, give each bit a weight. From left to right, the weights are 128, 64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2 and 1.

15
00:02:12,572 --> 00:02:16,775
Here is the first byte, 1 1 0 1 1 1 1 1.

16
00:02:17,225 --> 00:02:27,571
Add the weights of the 1 bits. 128 plus 64 plus 16 plus 8 plus 4 plus 2 plus 1 is 223.

17
00:02:28,021 --> 00:02:39,947
The other three bytes are 0 0 0 0 0 0 0 1. Each one is 1. So the address is 223.1.1.1.

18
00:02:40,397 --> 00:02:54,797
The smallest byte is all zeros, which is 0. The largest byte is all ones, which is 255. So each number in a dotted-decimal address is between 0 and 255.

19
00:02:55,247 --> 00:03:06,297
You must also convert in the other direction, from decimal to binary. Take the number 140. Go through the weights from 128 down to 1.

20
00:03:06,747 --> 00:03:16,177
128 fits into 140. So the first bit is 1. 140 minus 128 leaves 12.

21
00:03:16,627 --> 00:03:24,456
64, 32 and 16 do not fit into 12. So the next three bits are 0. 12 is still left.

22
00:03:24,906 --> 00:03:28,447
8 fits into 12. Write 1, and 4 is left.

23
00:03:28,897 --> 00:03:32,759
Then 4 fits into 4. Write 1, and 0 is left.

24
00:03:33,209 --> 00:03:37,582
Nothing is left. So the last two bits, for 2 and 1, are 0.

25
00:03:38,032 --> 00:03:47,653
The result is 1 0 0 0 1 1 0 0. Check it: 128 plus 8 plus 4 is 140.

26
00:03:48,103 --> 00:03:58,962
What is a subnet? A subnet is a set of interfaces that can physically reach each other without passing through a router. A subnet is a set of IP addresses.

27
00:03:59,412 --> 00:04:09,631
This network has three subnets. In each shaded area, the interfaces are on the same link. A packet between two of them does not go through the router.

28
00:04:10,081 --> 00:04:25,014
An IP address has a structure. The interfaces in the same subnet have the same high-order bits. This is the subnet part. In the left subnet, all four addresses start with 223.1.1.

29
00:04:25,464 --> 00:04:32,205
The remaining low-order bits are the host part. The host part is different for each interface in the subnet.

30
00:04:32,655 --> 00:04:39,738
The slides give a recipe to find the subnets. First, detach each interface from its host or router.

31
00:04:40,188 --> 00:04:46,289
Here the router is removed from its three links. The red circles show the detached interfaces.

32
00:04:46,739 --> 00:05:09,673
Each isolated island of the network is one subnet. Here there are three islands, so there are three subnets: 223.1.1.0/24, 223.1.2.0/24 and 223.1.3.0/24.

33
00:05:10,123 --> 00:05:18,187
The slash 24 is the subnet mask. It says that the higher-order 24 bits are the subnet part of the address.

34
00:05:18,637 --> 00:05:24,461
The other 32 minus 24, which is 8 lower-order bits, are the host part.

35
00:05:24,911 --> 00:05:31,908
8 host bits give 2 to the power 8, which is 256 IP addresses in each subnet.

36
00:05:32,358 --> 00:05:37,201
On the slide, this number prints as 2 8. It means 2 to the power 8.

37
00:05:37,651 --> 00:05:49,213
Here is a larger network from slide 32. It has three routers. Each router has three interfaces. Find the subnets, and give their slash 24 addresses.

38
00:05:49,663 --> 00:06:09,311
Use the recipe. Detach every interface. Three islands have hosts on them. They are the subnets 223.1.1/24, 223.1.2/24 and 223.1.3/24.

39
00:06:09,761 --> 00:06:31,820
But there are more islands. Each link between two routers is also isolated. So each link is a subnet with two interfaces. They are 223.1.9/24, 223.1.7/24 and 223.1.8/24.

40
00:06:32,270 --> 00:06:40,718
So this network has 3 plus 3, which is six subnets. A common mistake is to count only the three subnets with hosts.

41
00:06:41,168 --> 00:06:44,901
The slide says that these addresses are only an example.

42
00:06:45,351 --> 00:07:03,357
Before 1993, the Internet used classful addressing. The prefix length was fixed by the first bits of the address. A class A address begins with 0. Its prefix is 8 bits, and 32 minus 8, which is 24 bits, are for hosts.

43
00:07:03,807 --> 00:07:08,969
A class B address begins with 1 0. Its prefix is 16 bits.

44
00:07:09,419 --> 00:07:26,379
A class C address begins with 1 1 0. Its prefix is 24 bits, and 8 bits are for hosts. From the leading bits, you can find the range of the first byte. Class C starts at 192 and ends at 223.

45
00:07:26,829 --> 00:07:36,237
Class D begins with 1 1 1 0. Class E begins with 1 1 1 1. The slide prints one extra 1 for class E.

46
00:07:36,687 --> 00:07:44,666
The drawback is that the sizes are fixed. There is a fixed number of networks, and each network has a fixed number of addresses.

47
00:07:45,116 --> 00:07:59,345
A class C network has 2 to the power 8, which is 256 addresses. The next size, class B, has 2 to the power 16, which is 65,536.

48
00:07:59,795 --> 00:08:14,857
An organization with 2000 hosts gets a class B. 65,536 minus 2000 leaves 63,536 addresses that are not used.

49
00:08:15,307 --> 00:08:33,504
Since 1993, the Internet uses CIDR, classless interdomain routing. It is pronounced cider. With CIDR, the subnet part of an address can have any length. Here is the address 200.23.16.0 in binary.

50
00:08:33,954 --> 00:08:45,197
In this example, the subnet part is 23 bits long. The subnet part is also called the network prefix. The number of bits in the prefix is the prefix length.

51
00:08:45,647 --> 00:08:50,831
The remaining 32 minus 23, which is 9 bits, are the host part.

52
00:08:51,281 --> 00:08:59,558
We write the address and the prefix length together: 200.23.16.0/23.

53
00:09:00,008 --> 00:09:06,280
The general format is a dot b dot c dot d, slash x. Here x is 23.

54
00:09:06,730 --> 00:09:12,383
The 9 host bits give 2 to the power 9, which is 512 addresses.

55
00:09:12,833 --> 00:09:26,849
They go from 200.23.16.0 to 200.23.17.255. With CIDR, a network can get a block of the size it needs.

56
00:09:27,299 --> 00:09:41,571
For discussion, we write a subnet with its prefix length, for example 129.97.0.0/16. Routers use a different notation: the network mask.

57
00:09:42,021 --> 00:09:53,520
A network mask is the prefix length written in IP address style. For a prefix length of 16, the first 16 bits are 1 and the other 16 bits are 0.

58
00:09:53,970 --> 00:10:01,138
In dotted decimal, this is 255.255.0.0.

59
00:10:01,588 --> 00:10:15,497
So a router stores the destination network as 129.97.0.0, with the mask 255.255.0.0.

60
00:10:15,947 --> 00:10:31,222
The slide asks: is 255.255.64.0 a valid mask? Look at the third byte. 64 is 0 1 0 0 0 0 0 0.

61
00:10:31,672 --> 00:10:48,184
The third byte starts with a 0, and a 1 comes after it. A mask must be one block of 1s, then one block of 0s. So 255.255.64.0 is not a valid mask.

62
00:10:48,634 --> 00:10:58,789
Because a mask is a block of 1s followed by 0s, each byte of a mask can have only nine values. Each new 1 adds the weight of its position.

63
00:10:59,239 --> 00:11:01,521
With 0 ones, the byte is 0.

64
00:11:01,971 --> 00:11:04,723
With 1 one, it is 128.

65
00:11:05,173 --> 00:11:10,485
With 2 ones, 128 plus 64 is 192.

66
00:11:10,935 --> 00:11:16,333
With 3 ones, 192 plus 32 is 224.

67
00:11:16,783 --> 00:11:21,881
With 4 ones, 224 plus 16 is 240.

68
00:11:22,331 --> 00:11:26,982
With 5 ones, 240 plus 8 is 248.

69
00:11:27,432 --> 00:11:32,424
With 6 ones, 248 plus 4 is 252.

70
00:11:32,874 --> 00:11:37,802
With 7 ones, 252 plus 2 is 254.

71
00:11:38,252 --> 00:11:43,329
With 8 ones, 254 plus 1 is 255.

72
00:11:43,779 --> 00:11:46,531
Any other value in a mask is not valid.

73
00:11:46,981 --> 00:12:05,968
To write the mask for a prefix length, fill bytes of 8 ones first. For slash 20, 20 is 8 plus 8 plus 4. The byte with 4 ones is 240. So the mask is 255.255.240.0.

74
00:12:06,418 --> 00:12:19,431
Each subnet has an ID, the network ID. It has the form of an IP address together with a mask. This subnet is 10.2.5.16/28.

75
00:12:19,881 --> 00:12:27,049
Network IDs appear in routing tables. Normally, the addresses of individual hosts do not.

76
00:12:27,499 --> 00:12:33,686
For subnets that connect directly to a router, the system administrator configures them by hand.

77
00:12:34,136 --> 00:12:45,592
Each network also has a broadcast address. If you send an IP packet with the broadcast address as its destination, the packet goes to all the nodes on the network.

78
00:12:46,042 --> 00:12:57,114
Two protocols that use IP broadcast are DHCP, which we see in the next video, and ARP. ARP finds the MAC address for an IP address.

79
00:12:57,564 --> 00:13:09,319
Here is the recap. An IPv4 address has 32 bits. It identifies an interface, not a device. We write it as four bytes in dotted decimal.

80
00:13:09,769 --> 00:13:22,398
A subnet is a set of interfaces that reach each other without a router. To find subnets, detach the interfaces. Each island is a subnet. Links between routers are subnets too.

81
00:13:22,848 --> 00:13:31,189
With CIDR, the prefix length x can be any value. The address has x prefix bits and 32 minus x host bits.

82
00:13:31,639 --> 00:13:38,210
The mask has x ones, then zeros. Each byte of a valid mask is one of nine values.

83
00:13:38,660 --> 00:13:48,964
In video 14b, we compute network IDs and broadcast addresses, and we solve questions 29 to 45 of the question bank.
